        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Manifest destiny</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1845 CE – c. 1900 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val">John O’Sullivan, the newspaper man credited by most historians with coining the term in 1845; Jane Cazneau, whom some historians suggest wrote the unsigned editorial in which it first appeared; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> set the stage for continental expansion; John Quincy Adams, an early proponent of continentalism; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>, who spoke of “extending the area of freedom”; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-k-polk">James K. Polk</a>, president during the Oregon and Mexican settlements; Josiah Strong, whose 1885 book revived the idea; Albert J. Beveridge, who defended the acquisitions of 1898</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">John O’Sullivan’s 1839 article predicting a “divine destiny” for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; his essay “Annexation” in the Democratic Review (1845) and his editorial in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Morning News (27 December 1845); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-paine">Thomas Paine</a>’s Common Sense (1776); John Quincy Adams’s letter to his father (1811); Josiah Strong’s Our Country (1885); the historians Frederick Merk’s Manifest Destiny and Mission in American History (1963) and Reginald Horsman’s Race and Manifest Destiny (1981)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">the unique moral virtue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; a mission to spread republican government and the American way of life; a divinely ordained destiny; continentalism, the expectation that one nation would fill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> racial predominance; the Monroe Doctrine’s warning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; Native peoples expected to assimilate, move or vanish</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">The phrase declined in use after the turn of the 20th century. Wilson used it in 1920 for a mission to lead the cause of democracy, and critics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> foreign policy use it for interventions abroad. In 2025 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donald-trump">Donald Trump</a> used it in an inaugural address, the first president to do so in one.</span></div>
