    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The phrase was coined in 1845 by John O’Sullivan, or, some historians suggest, by Jane Cazneau; it became popular only after Whig opponents of the Polk administration criticised it.</li><li>There was never a set of principles defining manifest destiny: it was always a general idea, and its proponents held divergent and sometimes conflicting views, from territorial conquest to the call of a nation setting an example.</li><li>It was bound up with the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, the Oregon boundary and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>, and with the removal of Native Americans from their land; critics at the time and since have condemned it as a justification of dispossession.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>John O’Sullivan’s original idea was not a call for expansion by force: he expected Americans to settle new regions, set up democratic governments and then ask for admission to the Union, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> had done, and he predicted that even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> would one day ask.</p></aside>
    </section>

