    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1803</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> doubles the size of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1819</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Transcontinental Treaty</a> transfers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and extends the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> border with Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to the Pacific</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1830</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> signs the Indian Removal Act</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1845</span><span class="rf-text">John O’Sullivan uses the phrase “manifest destiny” in the Democratic Review, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> annexes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1846</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-treaty">Oregon Treaty</a> divides the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-country">Oregon Country</a> along the 49th parallel, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1848</span><span class="rf-text">The Mexican Cession adds Alta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Nuevo México</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1862</span><span class="rf-text">The Homestead Act offers western land to settlers for almost nothing</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mckinley">William McKinley</a> annexes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> brings the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and Guam</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-winthrop.jpg" alt="John Winthrop" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop">John Winthrop</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Winthrop_17th_cent._American_Antiquarian_Society_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author; when donated to the AAS, it was thought to be by a follower of Anthony van Dyck …, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Manifest destiny was the expansionist belief in the 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> that American settlers were destined to expand westward across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and that this destiny was both obvious (“manifest”) and certain. It is rooted in American exceptionalism, romantic nationalism, Anglo-Saxonism and nascent ideas of white chauvinism. The first of its themes, the unique virtue of the United States, was often traced to the Puritan heritage and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop">John Winthrop</a>’s “City upon a Hill” sermon of 1630, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-paine">Thomas Paine</a> echoed it in Common Sense in 1776, writing that “we have it in our power to begin the world over again”.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> looked forward to a time when the nation’s “rapid multiplication” would “cover the whole northern, if not the southern continent”, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> of 1803 set the stage for continental expansion. John Quincy Adams wrote to his father in 1811 that North America seemed “destined by Divine Providence to be peopled by one nation”. As secretary of state he orchestrated the Treaty of 1818, negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Transcontinental Treaty</a> of 1819 and formulated the Monroe Doctrine of 1823.</p><p>Most historians credit the newspaper editor John O’Sullivan with coining the term in 1845, in an essay titled “Annexation” in the Democratic Review urging the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>; some suggest instead that the unsigned editorial was written by the journalist Jane Cazneau. His second use, on 27 December 1845, claimed the whole of Oregon.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Manifest destiny was never a set of principles; it was a general idea, ill-defined but keenly felt. The historian William Earl Weeks names three tenets: the unique moral virtue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, a mission to redeem the world by spreading republican government and the “American way of life”, and faith in a divinely ordained destiny to succeed in that mission. Proponents offered divergent or conflicting views. Many writers concentrated on expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> or across the Pacific, while others took the term as a call to example. Ernest Lee Tuveson wrote that the “vast complex of ideas, policies, and actions” under the phrase were “not, as we should expect, all compatible”.</p><p>O’Sullivan saw manifest destiny as a moral ideal, a “higher law”, that overruled other claims. He believed that expansion would happen without the government or the army: Americans would immigrate, set up democratic governments and seek admission to the Union. Continentalism, the belief that the nation would eventually fill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, was one strand; the Monroe Doctrine was another, and the historian Walter McDougall calls manifest destiny a corollary of it, since expansion was needed to enforce it. Trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> was a further motive.</p><p>Racial ideas also played a part. The historian Reginald Horsman wrote that a view of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> race as innately superior held that “inferior races were doomed to subordinate status or extinction”, and that it was used to justify the enslavement of Black people and the expulsion and possible extermination of Indians.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-mckinley"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-mckinley.jpg" alt="William McKinley" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/william-mckinley">William McKinley</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:McKinley_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The phrase is most associated with the territorial expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1803 to 1900, though some scholars limit the age of manifest destiny to the continental expansion from the Louisiana Purchase to the purchase of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> in 1867. In the 1840s it took the form of calls for “All Oregon” and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> began in 1846, for “All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>”. Slogans such as “Fifty-four forty or fight” expressed the first. Polk instead renewed an offer to divide Oregon along the 49th parallel, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-treaty">Oregon Treaty</a> of 1846 settled it that way.</p><p>After 1848 the disagreement over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> made further annexation by conquest too divisive to be official policy, and the most radical advocates turned to filibustering, private military expeditions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and Narciso López in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> were the best known; the Democratic national platform endorsed Walker’s expedition. The Ostend Manifesto of 1854, which argued that the United States could seize Cuba by force, effectively killed the effort to acquire it.</p><p>After the Civil War the idea revived. Josiah Strong’s Our Country (1885) argued that the future fell to America, and the Republican platform of 1892 spoke of “the manifest destiny of the Republic in its broadest sense”. In 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-mckinley">William McKinley</a> said of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, “It is manifest destiny.” Historians have disagreed whether the acquisitions of the 1890s extended manifest destiny across the Pacific or were its antithesis and merely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant.jpg" alt="Ulysses S. Grant" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulysses_S._Grant_1870-1880_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brady-Handy Photograph Collection, Library of Congress, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Democrats used the concept to justify the Oregon claim and the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> in 1845 as a slave state, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> in 1846. The war ended with the Mexican Cession, which added Alta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Nuevo México</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a>, who served in it, called it “one of the most unjust ever waged by a stronger against a weaker nation”.</p><p>The consequences for Native Americans were serious, since continental expansion implicitly meant the occupation and annexation of their land. The federal government bought land through treaties, and its policy was that Indians should join American society and become “civilized”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> believed they had to assimilate or be pushed aside, and the historian Jeffrey Ostler holds that he advocated extermination once he thought assimilation was no longer possible. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> signed the Indian Removal Act in 1830. After the Civil War the army waged wars on “non-treaty Indians”, and the Homestead Act of 1862 in some areas resulted in the direct removal of Indigenous communities. Hunting reduced the bison to a few hundred. Francis Parkman wrote in 1851 that Indians were “destined to melt and vanish”.</p><p>In 1898 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and Guam under United States rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a> followed in 1899, against a revolutionary government that sought independence. These islands were acquired as colonies rather than prospective states, a step validated by the Insular Cases.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln.jpg" alt="Abraham Lincoln" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abraham_Lincoln_O-77_matte_collodion_print.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexander Gardner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Opposition began at once. The Whigs denounced the doctrine, one resolution calling its supporters traitors to the Constitution for “preaching the doctrine of the right of conquest”. Robert Winthrop was the first to use the phrase in Congress, on 3 January 1846, mocking “the right of our manifest destiny” and beginning a long line of critics who argued that its advocates cited Providence for actions driven by chauvinism and self-interest. John Quincy Adams repudiated expansionism by 1843 because it meant extending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> opposed the Mexican war and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> of manifest destiny as unjust.</p><p>John C. Calhoun opposed annexing all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on racial grounds: “Ours, sir, is the Government of a white race.” The debate showed a contradiction. The mission suggested Mexicans would be improved by joining American democracy, while racial ideas held that non-whites were unfit to be Americans. Some proponents of annexing all of Mexico regarded it as an anti-slavery measure.</p><p>Frederick Merk argued in 1963 that manifest destiny had less support than historians portrayed, and that aggressive continentalism was an aberration backed by a minority. Critics have condemned it as an ideology used to justify the dispossession of and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> against Indigenous peoples. Two Native American writers, Nick Estes and Tonya Gonnella Frichner, link it to the Doctrine of Christian Discovery. William Jennings Bryan, opposing the Philippine war, wrote that “‘Destiny’ is not as manifest as it was a few weeks ago.”</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt.jpg" alt="Theodore Roosevelt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodore_Roosevelt_by_the_Pach_Bros_(4x5_cropped)_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adam Cuerden, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the turn of the 20th century the phrase declined in use as territorial expansion stopped being promoted as part of America’s destiny. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a>’s corollary to the Monroe Doctrine in 1904 defined the nation as an “international police power” in the Western Hemisphere and explicitly rejected territorial expansion, so that interventionism replaced expansionism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">President Wilson</a> tried to redefine the phrase on a worldwide scale; in 1920 he called it “the manifest destiny of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to lead” in making democracy prevail, the only time a president had used it in an annual message.</p><p>The belief in an American mission to promote and defend democracy, expounded by Jefferson and continued by Lincoln, Wilson and George W. Bush, still influences American political ideology. Critics of United States foreign policy use “manifest destiny” for interventions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and elsewhere, while scholars at the end of the 20th century devised the more positive-sounding “nation building”.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> and Nazi ideologues studied American westward expansion, including its legal techniques of land seizure and population displacement, as a model for their conquest and ethnic cleansing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, and the idea directly inspired the concept of Lebensraum. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> adopted the phrase to justify its own expansion; Iris Chang wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was twentieth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>’s manifest destiny. In 2025 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donald-trump">Donald Trump</a> used the phrase in his inaugural address.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Manifest destiny was the belief, named in 1845, that American settlers were destined to expand westward across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. It was never a fixed doctrine, and it shaped the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, the Oregon and Mexican settlements and the removal of Native peoples, while Whigs, Lincoln and later critics condemned it as a cover for conquest and dispossession.</em></p>

